  "problem": "Expressed in radians, $\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061(0)=${{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14036892/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(0)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061(0)=0.\n$$",
